## clean site build — ONE unambiguous command, from the repo root

    cd site && rm -rf dist && npm run build

(The previous record showed `rm -rf site/dist && npm run build` — ambiguous: from
`site/` it removes `site/site/dist`, and from the repo root `npm run build` builds the
CLI, not the site. The command above is what was actually executed; it resolves only
from the repo root, which is exactly how `cd site && …` is read.)

`rm -rf dist` is load-bearing: the clean must be real or stale files survive.

    build exit=0
    8 page(s) built in 720ms

Old `node:crypto` externalization warning ABSENT, with a positive control on the SAME
log (a real successful build log always prints `built in`). `grep -c` prints an explicit
0 for a no-match, so the absence is a number, not a silenced command:

    $ grep -c 'externalized for browser compatibility' <build log>      -> 0   (absent)
    $ grep -c 'built in'                        <build log>      -> 3   (same-log positive control)

## emitted browser JS — DISCRIMINATING sentinel probe

Scan `site/dist/_astro/*.js` for root-only release metadata that used to leak in via
`agent-prompt.ts -> install.ts -> release.ts -> root package.json / src/cloud/config.ts`.
`rg` applies the alternation as a REGEX; `-l` lists matching files; piping to `wc -l`
prints an explicit count (0 for no matches). The probe is enumerated per sentinel and
combined:

    $ for s in 'build:command-core' 'test:p1-server' 'node:crypto'; do
    >   echo "$s -> $(rg -l -e "$s" site/dist/_astro --glob '*.js' | wc -l | tr -d ' ') file(s)"
    > done
    build:command-core -> 0 file(s)
    test:p1-server -> 0 file(s)
    node:crypto -> 0 file(s)

    $ rg -l -e 'build:command-core' -e 'test:p1-server' -e 'node:crypto' site/dist/_astro --glob '*.js' | wc -l
    0

POSITIVE CONTROL, same probe shape — the scanner reads real emitted JS (a string that
must be in the built bundles):

    $ rg -l 'navigator.clipboard' site/dist/_astro --glob '*.js' | wc -l
    3

(The old record used `grep -c 'build:command-core|test:p1-server|node:crypto'` — basic
grep treats `|` as LITERAL text, so that probe reported 0 even when a sentinel was
present. It is superseded; the mutation that proves the rg probe CAN turn red is in the
red log.)

